<Base title={`${title} | SubFixer`} description="Chromecast often skips a plain SRT file. Convert the subtitles to WebVTT, keep the letters in UTF-8, and cast again. The converter runs in your browser." path="/guides/chromecast-subtitles-not-showing/" jsonld={jsonld} ogType="article">
  <article class="guide">
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">You cast a movie from your phone or computer, the picture plays, and no subtitles appear. The file itself is often fine. The Chromecast reads a short list of subtitle formats, and a plain SRT file is not one of them, so the app that sends the video has to convert the file, or the subtitles may not show. This guide explains how to check that and how to convert the file to WebVTT, which the device does read.</p>

    <h2>What the Chromecast expects</h2>
    <p>A Chromecast does not open a subtitle file on its own. The app that sends the video, such as a phone app or a media player on your computer, sends the picture together with a subtitle track to the device. The Google Cast media documentation lists WebVTT and TTML as the text subtitle formats that Cast supports. SRT is not on that list, so a plain SRT file often needs converting before the device can show it.</p>
    <p>Some apps convert SRT to WebVTT for you. Others send the file as it is, and then the subtitles do not appear. You often cannot tell from the app's menus which one it does, so the safest route is to give the app a WebVTT file. That works in both cases.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: Check that the file is really SRT</h2>
    <p>Open the file in a plain text editor such as Notepad or TextEdit. A real SRT starts with a number, then a time line with commas in the times, like <code>00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,500</code>. Look at the first line of the file:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If it says <code>WEBVTT</code>, the file is already WebVTT. It only needs the .vtt ending.</li>
      <li>If it says <code>[Script Info]</code>, the file is an ASS subtitle with an .srt name.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>On Windows, known file endings are hidden by default. A file you saved as <code>Movie.srt</code> may really be <code>Movie.srt.txt</code>, and an app will not read it as a subtitle file. Turn on "File name extensions" in the View tab of File Explorer to see the real name, then rename the file so it ends in .srt.</p>
    <p>Renamed files are common, and our tools handle them. The tools read the content of a file, not its name, so a VTT or ASS file with an .srt name still opens in the <a href="/srt-to-vtt/">SRT to VTT converter</a>. The converter detects the real format and writes a clean WebVTT file.</p>

    <h2>Step 2: Convert the file to WebVTT</h2>
    <p>Open the <a href="/srt-to-vtt/">SRT to VTT converter</a>, drop in the file, and download the result. The output is already set to WebVTT, so there is no format to choose. Two changes matter for the device:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The first line of the file becomes <code>WEBVTT</code>, followed by a blank line.</li>
      <li>The times use dots instead of commas, so <code>00:00:01,000</code> becomes <code>00:00:01.000</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Here is a short file before and after the conversion. The text lines stay the same. The <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> tags are kept, because WebVTT supports them too.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <pre class="sample"><code>Before (SRT)
1
00:00:01,000 --&gt; 00:00:02,500
&lt;i&gt;Hello&lt;/i&gt;

2
00:00:04,000 --&gt; 00:00:06,000
What time is it?

After (WebVTT)
WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --&gt; 00:00:02.500
&lt;i&gt;Hello&lt;/i&gt;

2
00:00:04.000 --&gt; 00:00:06.000
What time is it?</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>Open the converted file in a text editor before you use it. The first line should read <code>WEBVTT</code>, and the times should use dots. If the first line is still a number and the times still have commas, the file was not converted, so start again from the original SRT.</p>
    <p>Give the converted file a name that matches the video, such as <code>Movie.en.vtt</code>, so it is easy to tell apart from the original.</p>

    <h2>Step 3: Keep accented letters intact</h2>
    <p>Accented letters and non-Latin scripts can go wrong during a cast. Users often report that letters outside plain English vanished until the file was saved as UTF-8. A common cause is an SRT saved in an old Windows encoding, so the letters are read wrong before the device ever sees them.</p>
    <p>Our downloads are saved as UTF-8. The converter builds the file from a text string in your browser, and browsers save those strings as UTF-8. The letters in the .vtt file are the same letters you see in the converted text.</p>
    <p>If the letters in your original file are already wrong, fix them first with the <a href="/fix-subtitle-encoding/">fix subtitle encoding</a> tool. It reads a file in a chosen or guessed encoding and saves it as UTF-8. Then convert the fixed file to WebVTT, so the device gets clean text from the start.</p>

    <h2>Check the file before you convert it</h2>
    <p>Run the SRT through the <a href="/srt-validator/">SRT validator</a> before you convert it. The validator flags overlapping lines and numbers that are out of order, among other problems. Each problem is listed with the line it sits on, so you can fix it in the original file. Fixing those first means you are not guessing later which step caused a problem.</p>

    <h2>When the file comes from a web address</h2>
    <p>Some apps do not send the subtitle file from your device. They give the Chromecast a web address, and the device fetches the file from there. The Google Cast documentation says that subtitle files need to be served with CORS headers in that case. CORS is a setting on the web server that tells browsers and devices which other sites may fetch a file.</p>
    <p>If you run that server, make sure the .vtt file is sent with CORS allowed. If you do not, the fix is in the app, so contact its developer or try a different app.</p>

    <h2>TTML is the other text format</h2>
    <p>The Cast documentation also lists TTML. TTML is an XML format, and some streaming and broadcast workflows ask for it. The <a href="/srt-to-ttml/">SRT to TTML converter</a> keeps the timing and text of your file in that format. For a film or show you copied yourself, WebVTT is usually the shorter route, and the same .vtt file also plays in web browsers.</p>

    <h2>If it plays on a computer but not on the Chromecast</h2>
    <p>Play the same video with the same subtitle file in another player on your computer. If the subtitles show there, the file is fine, and the difference is in how the cast app hands the track to the device. Look for a subtitle or CC setting in the cast app, or try a different app. If the subtitles still do not show, check the original file with the validator, fix anything it lists, and convert it again.</p>

    <p>See also: <a href="/guides/srt-vtt-ass/">SRT, VTT and ASS: what is the difference?</a>, which explains why these formats look alike and fail in different ways.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
